@meta
id: 30-10
title: Drill — clear the diagnostics
chapter: 30
type: drill
skills: diagnostics
par: 58
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: click the first problem, retype, click the next
vimrc: set errorformat=%-G\ %.%#,%f(%l\\,%c):\ error\ %m
open: src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts

@brief
Two misspelled property names, both with a "did you mean" in the
message. `tsc.log` is provided with the lesson and does not change.

Eight times: load the list, fix the word under the cursor, next, fix,
save and quit.

@keys
| `:cfile file` | load the errors and jump to the first |
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |
| `:cn` | next error |

@hints
`:cfile tsc.log` puts the cursor on the exact character the compiler
complained about, so `ciw` is already aimed.
--
Do not read the file to find the second error. `:cn` knows where it is.

@solution
    :cfile tsc.log<Enter>  ciw sendEmail <Esc>  :cn<Enter>  ciw customerId <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

The rhythm — load, `:cn`, fix, `:cn`, fix — is the same whether the
list came from a saved log, from `:make`, from `:grep`, or from a
language server plugin pushing diagnostics into it. Quickfix is the
common currency. Learn the rhythm once and every tool that can produce
a list of file-line-message fits into it.

@start tsc.log
src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts(9,29): error TS2551: Property 'sendEmial' does not exist on type 'EmailService'. Did you mean 'sendEmail'?
  src/infrastructure/services/email.service.ts(7,3): 'sendEmail' is declared here.
src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts(15,47): error TS2551: Property 'custmerId' does not exist on type '{ customerId: string; }'. Did you mean 'customerId'?

@start src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(protected emailService: EmailService) {}

  public async welcome(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(customerId);

    await this.emailService.sendEmial(customer.email);

    return customer;
  }

  public async notify(order: { customerId: string }) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(order.custmerId);

    return this.emailService.sendReceipt(customer.email);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(protected emailService: EmailService) {}

  public async welcome(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(customerId);

    await this.emailService.sendEmail(customer.email);

    return customer;
  }

  public async notify(order: { customerId: string }) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(order.customerId);

    return this.emailService.sendReceipt(customer.email);
  }
}
